WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.030
MARTIN: Hey, Josh, where
did you get that lollipop?

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.740
JOSH: Oh, hi, Martin.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.522
The Slip Slap Candy
Company came by

00:00:06.522 --> 00:00:08.189
and asked for help
connecting their code

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:09.720
to a Google spreadsheet.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.210
I told them, we might
have something for you.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.830
MARTIN: Sounds great!

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.079
Stay tuned if you
want to know more.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:18.474
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.670
JOSH: I met with Max from
Slip Slap Candy Company.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.740
He explained that many
of their internal systems

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.410
need to know what ingredients
they have in their warehouse.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.360
MARTIN: It turns out,
the warehouse staff

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.600
already keeps track of
this in a Google Sheet.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.420
That's great news, because it's
easy to read a Google Sheet

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:42.820
from Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:44.840
JOSH: Max didn't want to
deal with server setup

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.760
and maintenance, so
he went serverless

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.230
and picked Cloud Run.

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:50.930
And he has previous
experience in JavaScript.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.210
So we decided to
use Node.js together

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:54.680
with the Express web framework.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.700
MARTIN: Cloud Run code executes
as a default identity--

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.850
the compute default
service account.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:04.080
Max opened the
warehouse spreadsheet,

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.400
clicked the Share button, and
gave that account view access

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.270
to the spreadsheet.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:12.905
JOSH: Then he enabled the Sheets
API in Slip Slap's GCP project.

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:15.650
MARTIN: Max designed
a really simple REST

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.380
API with only one endpoint.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:21.320
If you hit slash
Ingredient ID, you

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.245
will get the warehouse
details for that ingredient.

00:01:24.245 --> 00:01:25.820
JOSH: The Express
Library makes it

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:28.850
easy to pick up the ingredient
ID and take action on it.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:31.370
MARTIN: Once Max has
the ingredient ID,

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:33.620
he can call the
getIngredient function

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:37.520
and return that ingredient
to the caller in JSON form.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.770
JOSH: The code for reading
from the Google Sheet

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.240
is in the
getIngredient function.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.200
It creates an auth object
with a spreadsheet scope.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.320
It then reads columns A
through E for all rows that

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.890
have any data.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.890
Notice how there are
no passwords, keys,

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:53.510
or oauth secrets.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:56.720
The account running this code
already has read-only access

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:57.650
to the sheet.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.320
MARTIN: Once the Sheets
API returns the rows

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.160
from the sheet, Max can loop
over them, find the ingredient

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.280
with the right ID, and
return that ingredient's data

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.110
to the calling function.

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:11.300
Check the video description
below for the link

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.890
to the full code example.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:15.320
JOSH: This code reads
all rows in the sheet

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:17.780
every time the
REST API is called.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.300
That's OK for a small
sheet like this.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.960
Slip Slap only has about 1,000
ingredients in their sheet.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.700
If you have a much
larger data set,

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.170
you should use a
regular database,

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.145
like Cloud Firestore
or Cloud SQL.

00:02:29.145 --> 00:02:31.970
MARTIN: As the warehouse
staff updates the spreadsheet,

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:35.690
the REST API will always
return the latest data.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:38.000
Max loves this, as
that means he doesn't

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.000
have to write a separate
admin user interface.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.860
JOSH: That's less
than 50 lines of code

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.200
for a simple read-only
REST API that's

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.760
backed by a Google Sheet.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.320
Nice.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:51.950
Well, Max gave me
this US $1 bill.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.470
He wonders, how much
could Slip Slap use

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:56.315
this service for that dollar?

00:02:56.315 --> 00:02:58.940
MARTIN: Let's say Slip
Slap's internal systems hit

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:02.330
this service on average
once every 30 seconds, 24

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.950
hours a day, seven days a week.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.190
This would fit comfortably
within the free tier

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:10.430
for Cloud Run, and they
wouldn't have to pay anything.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:13.100
But what if they've used
up their free tier running

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.200
other Cloud Run services?

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.190
In that case, it would cost
Slip Slap a little under $1 US

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:21.650
to run the service
for one month.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:22.970
JOSH: Not bad.

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:24.740
I'm also sure Max
is happy he doesn't

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:26.390
have to manage any servers.

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:28.520
MARTIN: Yeah, I think so.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:31.550
Josh, Max didn't give you
another one of those lollipops,

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:34.550
by any chance?

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:37.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

